DS (relation_snapshot store-read-failure), not shown to be a `git merge` abort with the local-changes signature"],"finding":"The plan's deletion-recovery mechanism and its RED fixture assume a WORKTREE deletion (` D`) of a tracked file makes `git merge` abort with 'Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge', but git generally aborts on that signature for MODIFICATIONS and STAGED deletions — not a clean unstaged worktree deletion, which a merge tends to restore silently. If the fixture only unstages the deletion, the RED test may not actually abort (fails to be RED), and the fix may not target the state that actually wedges the store.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f3c1d650f91fa2bc6","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach; Testing (RED-first fixture)","norm_id":"nbcb800bc5e17e503","priority":0.0978,"reason":"veto:plan-goal-satisfied","recalled":false,"scenarios":["RED fixture stages nothing and only deletes in the worktree: `git merge` restores the file and completes, so the test is never RED and the recovery seam is never exercised","The real wedge state is a STAGED deletion (`D `) left by the fold's `git add -A`, which the plan text does not name in its ` D`-only description"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"In the Approach and Testing sections, enumerate the porcelain states the recovery restores from HEAD — worktree deletion (` D`) AND staged deletion (`D `, the `git add -A` residue named in the RCA) — and build the RED fixture from the staged-deletion state if that is what makes `git merge` abort, so the RED assertion is genuinely RED.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.2222,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"no","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"yes","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_exist